{"record": "turn", "turn": 4, "seconds": 3.354, "response_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "stop_reason": "tool_use", "stop_details": null, "blocks": ["tool_use"], "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 7519}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7519, "cache_read_input_tokens": 15395, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 423, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "tools": [{"id": "toolu_01TJvmJrsV9CwMdfPUSbVyaa", "name": "submit_answer", "arguments": {"answer": "Faintest excess: the X-shaped bulge, which is also the worst fit. In the ring-based fits its −2ΔlnL is +30 relative to no excess, so it is slightly worse than having no excess. Its Δln B is −190. Boxy-bulge candidates: the boxy bulge alone, boxy bulge \"plus\" (with the nuclear stellar bulge), and boxy + X-shaped bulge, all have very similar spectra. These spectra are relatively soft, which the paper says is compatible with the excess having absorbed unmodeled diffuse flux. Dark matter: with ring-based templates its spectrum is harder on average than those boxy-bulge spectra. It is similar in shape to the DM spectrum found with the astrophysics-based templates of Cholis et al., but lower in flux. It also shows \"wiggles\" between energy bins that neither DM annihilation nor millisecond pulsars predict.", "found": true, "evidence": [{"arxiv_id": "2209.00006v2", "section_id": "0"}]}, "status": "answer", "error_kind": null, "seconds": 0.0}]}
